  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nIt looks like quite an active few days across the area as my \nnemesis, the monsoon, with an assist from an upper level low, \nwill continue to bring rounds of showers and thunderstorms for \nthe next few days. We continue to have convection tonight, \nespecially in western Wyoming where some storms have been quite \nstrong. They should slowly weaken as we head into the night. \nHowever, many of the storms have produced very heavy rain back \nin Idaho and some localized flooding will be possible before \nsunrise, especially north of Jackson and into Yellowstone Park.\n\nAs for today, some showers and an isolated thunderstorm will be \naround in the morning. The main action looks to be this afternoon \nand tonight as we get some daytime heating to destabilize the \natmosphere and a shortwave around the parent upper level low \nbrings some additional lift in the afternoon and evening. The \nresult will be a good coverage of showers and thunderstorms \nacross the area. As for timing, the most numerous storms should \nbe west of the Divide and across southern Wyoming in the \nafternoon, then moving east of the Divide later in the \nafternoon and into the evening. There is fair model agreement in\nthe most numerous storms to be in the northern counties east of\nthe Divide this evening, with lessening chances further south. \nThis lines up well with the Flood Watch, which we will continue\nas is. Precipitable water values remain abnormally high, \ngenerally 150 to as much as 200 percent of normal, especially \neast of the Divide. So, as would be expected, any storm could \nproduce heavy rain and localized flooding. But there is another\naspect. There could be some strong to possible severe \nthunderstorms. There is close to 1000 J/kg of mid level CAPE as\nthe trough approaches the western portions of the state. There \nis obviously plenty of moisture. Directional shear will also be \nincreasing as the upper level low moves closer. The main threat \nother than heavy rain would be hail, especially with fairly low \nwet bulb zero levels. Showers and storms should continue into \nthe night, although coverage should gradually decrease.\n\nThe upper level low continues to drift eastward on Saturday, moving \ninto Wyoming later in the day. On this day, the most numerous \nshowers and storms will shift to northern Wyoming. Some drier air \nmay try to wrap into the southern portions of the area, possibly \nshutting off convection for a while. We have maintained at \nleast a 1 in 5 chance for all areas though. A better chance of \nshowers likely returns Saturday night as low pressure moves \nacross the area. Again, despite decent steering flow, the amount\nof moisture will be sufficient for locally heavy rain and \npossible flooding.\n\nAs we head into Sunday, conditions may begin to improve as the low \nmoves east into the Dakotas. There will still be some showers around \nfrom the wrap around moisture though. The main threat on this day \nshifts to northeastern portions of the area, with decreasing chances \nto the south and west, with mainly dry conditions expected across \nsouthwestern Wyoming. With flow turning northwest, it should be a \ncooler day as well. Showers should then end for all areas by Sunday \nevening.\n\nThere continues to be more uncertainty regarding next week. Some \nguidance brings another system up from the southwest with another \nchance of showers on Monday or Tuesday, others keep it south of the \narea, primarily impacting Utah and Colorado. Given the concerns in \nthe short term, we didn't have time to take a good look this far \nout. One thing we are more confident is that flow will turn \nsouth to southwesterly again, and bring another period of above \nnormal temperatures for the middle and end of next week.",
